# Frontend proxy

The nx-fhir `frontend-proxy` feature gives this server the endpoint `/proxy`. The integrated frontend sends each request for a different FHIR server to this endpoint, on the origin of the page. The server then sends the request to the destination, so the browser needs no CORS permission from the other server.

A request for this server goes directly to `/fhir`. A request for any other server goes to `/proxy?url=<destination>`, with the destination URL encoded.

## What the feature adds

The feature adds `FrontendProxyController`, `ProxyHttpClients`, `ProxyDestinations`, and `PublicAddresses` under `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/proxy/`, the unit tests `PublicAddressesTest`, `ProxyDestinationsTest`, `FrontendProxyControllerTest`, and `FhirServersTest` and the integration test `FrontendProxyControllerIT`, which runs under `mvn verify`, under `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/proxy/`. The install also adds the dependency `org.apache.httpcomponents.client5:httpclient5` to `pom.xml`, with the version that the HAPI FHIR parent pom manages, and `DEV_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:8080` to the `.env.development` of each integrated frontend that has no `DEV_SERVER_URL` line.

On HAPI 8.12, the `httpclient5` dependency makes Apache HttpClient 5 the default HTTP client of Spring Boot. The property `spring.http.client.factory=jdk` selects the JDK client for all other code.

## Configuration

| Key | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `app.proxy.enabled` | `false` when absent. The install writes `true`. | Master switch. When `false`, `/proxy` returns 404 and `/config.js` has no `proxy` object. |
| `app.proxy.passthrough` | `false` | Permits requests to servers that are not listed. Read the warning in the passthrough section first. |
| `app.proxy.connect-timeout` | `5s` | Connect timeout for both HTTP clients. |
| `app.proxy.read-timeout` | `60s` | Read timeout for both HTTP clients. |

Behind a reverse proxy that ends TLS, set `server.forward-headers-strategy`, for example to `framework`. The application then sees the scheme that the browser sent, and `/proxy` accepts the `Origin` header of the page.

### Listed servers

The proxy uses `app.fhir.servers`, the property that gives the frontend its known servers. Each entry with an absolute URL is a listed server. The proxy permits each listed server, internal hosts included, for example a service name in Docker Compose. A root-relative entry is this server, and the frontend requests it directly on the origin of the page.

The property is a YAML list of entries with a `name` and a `url`, or one string that holds a JSON array of `{ "name", "url" }` entries, which one environment variable can set. Set `app.fhir.servers` in one form only. A string form in any property source has priority over a list form, even when the list is in a source with higher priority.

The `url` of each entry must be an absolute `http` or `https` URL with a host, or a root-relative path such as `/fhir`. An absolute URL must have no query, no fragment, and no user information part. A root-relative path must have at least one character after the first `/`, and no query, no fragment, and no backslash. A host name with an underscore, for example `hapi_server`, is not valid for the Java URL parser and stops the application at startup.

The application also stops at startup when the string form is not valid, or when an entry has no `name` or no `url`. An empty or absent value is valid. `/config.js` sends only the `name` and the `url` of each entry to the browser.

## How the server classifies a destination

1. If the destination is under a listed server, the server forwards the request. A listed server can be an internal host.
2. If the destination is under no listed server and passthrough is on, the server forwards the request through the guard. The guard permits public addresses only.
3. In all other cases, the server refuses the request with status 403.

A destination is under a listed server when its scheme, host, and port are equal to those of the server URL, and its path is the base path or starts with the base path plus `/`. A destination on the same host but outside the base path counts as not listed.

The proxy forwards the custom headers that the user sets in the settings dialog to the destination. An upstream redirect gives status 502.

## Passthrough

WARNING: With `app.proxy.passthrough: true`, anyone who can reach the application can send requests through it to any public host. Use this only for a test deployment.

The guarded client connects to the destination directly and ignores the JVM HTTP proxy settings. A JVM SOCKS proxy setting (`socksProxyHost`) applies, and the SOCKS proxy connects to the address that the guard examined.

## Development mode

The Vite server of the frontend reads `DEV_SERVER_URL` from `.env.development` and forwards `/proxy`, `/config.js`, and `/fhir` to that address. Change the value if this server uses a port other than 8080.
